  "comments": [
    {
      "position": 0,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "The 'normal' negative sentence would be 私は肉を食べない。'I don't eat fish'.\nWith the object marker を overridden by は this implies that not eating meat is in contrast to eating something else (for example fish).\nNote: When Japanese refer to meat (肉) they generally would not understand that as including fish (魚).",
      "html": "The 'normal' negative sentence would be 私は肉を食べない。'I don't eat fish'.<br/>With the object marker を overridden by は this implies that not eating meat is in contrast to eating something else (for example fish).<br/>Note: When Japanese refer to meat (肉) they generally would not understand that as including fish (魚).",
      "credits_raw": "Amatuka",
      "content_sha256": "c5c866d809ccace353362e951a4c293a1364f832e6be3065d72c0b5d5589ef7c",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 1,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "This contrastative は can fit in places that you might not expect. Take 生きている '(she's) alive.' vs. 生きてはいる '(she's) alive (but not well).'.",
      "html": "This contrastative は can fit in places that you might not expect. Take 生きている '(she's) alive.' vs. 生きてはいる '(she's) alive (but not well).'.",
      "credits_raw": "Amatuka",
      "content_sha256": "c0e7bbaa1fe7587d8b5fd78e79cb49a8cf71147c1fe00346b53d8633cd9474ba",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
